  <title>Text, dates and serial numbers</title>

  <p><gui>+ Text</gui> adds a line of text. Select it, and type its text and
  set its size in the <link xref="properties-panel">Properties
  panel</link>. In ZPL it is a <code>^A</code> or <code>^A@</code> font
  command and the text in <code>^FD</code>.</p>

  <section id="measure">
    <title>Why the box is the width it is</title>
    <p>The box around text is always as wide as the text will print, because
    LinuxZPL measures it with the font the printer will use:</p>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title>The printer's built-in font (the default)</title>
        <p>New text uses the printer's font F, a bitmap font whose characters
        are drawn in a cell 26 dots tall and 13 wide, with a small gap
        between characters. A bitmap font can only be printed at whole
        multiples of its cell, 1 to 10 times in each direction, so the
        printer rounds the height and width you give it to the nearest
        multiple. Text set to <gui>Font Height</gui> 36 and
        <gui>Font Width</gui> 20 prints in a 26 × 26 cell, and the box shows
        that size, not 36 × 20. The panel keeps the numbers you typed.</p>
        <p>The cell is a fixed number of dots, so the same text prints
        smaller on a 300 dpi printer than on a 203 dpi one.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>Font 0</title>
        <p>The printer's scalable font, used by many other programs. LinuxZPL
        measures it with a close stand-in: see
        <link xref="fonts#font-zero"/>.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>A TrueType font</title>
        <p>Measured from the font file itself, letter by letter, so
        <code>IIII</code> is narrow and <code>WWWW</code> wide, as they
        print.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
    <p>That is why there is no <gui>Width</gui> row: the width follows from
    the text and the font. To make text wider, raise <gui>Font Width</gui>
    or drag a side handle.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="size">
    <title>Size, turn and direction</title>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Font Height</gui> and <gui>Font Width</gui></title>
        <p>In dots, 8 to 32000. The width stretches or narrows each
        character. Dragging a side handle changes the font width, and a top
        or bottom handle the height; the box then comes back to the size the
        text prints.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Orientation</gui></title>
        <p><gui>Normal</gui>, <gui>Rotated 90°</gui>, <gui>Upside down</gui>
        or <gui>Rotated 270°</gui>. Turned 90° or 270°, the box's width and
        height swap, and so do the handles' jobs: the top and bottom handles
        then change the font width, and the side handles the height.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Direction</gui> and <gui>Character Gap</gui></title>
        <p>Lay the characters out <gui>Left to right</gui>,
        <gui>Top to bottom</gui> or <gui>Right to left</gui>, with extra dots
        between them. Top to bottom stacks the characters upright, one font
        height apart, in a column as wide as its widest character.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Reverse</gui></title>
        <p>Prints the text white wherever something is already printed
        beneath it, and black elsewhere.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
  </section>

  <section id="font">
    <title>The font</title>
    <p><gui>Font</gui> shows <gui>Default</gui> until you press
    <gui>Choose…</gui> and pick one of the TrueType fonts installed on this
    computer. <gui>Clear</gui> goes back to the default, which is the
    printer's own built-in font unless the label names another.</p>
    <p>A chosen font has to be on the printer too. LinuxZPL checks that
    before printing, and offers to send it: see
    <link xref="fonts"/>.</p>
    <note>
      <p>The printer's built-in fonts are made of dots, and grow only in
      whole steps. Dragging such text larger jumps from size to size rather
      than growing smoothly. The canvas shows the size that will print.</p>
    </note>
  </section>

  <section id="characters">
    <title>Letters beyond plain English</title>
    <p>Text can hold any letter or symbol, such as é, ü, ß or €. When it
    does, LinuxZPL tells the printer the label is in Unicode (UTF-8,
    <code>^CI28</code>), which needs printer firmware V50.14, V60.14 or
    later. Whether a letter actually prints depends on the font having it.
    The printer's built-in bitmap fonts carry a limited set, so for anything
    unusual choose a TrueType font that has the letters, and let LinuxZPL
    send it to the printer.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="wrap">
    <title>Wrap text over several lines</title>
    <p>Press <key>Enter</key> in the text, or tick <gui>Wrap</gui>, and the
    text wraps inside a block. <gui>Block</gui> offers two kinds:</p>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Field block (^FB)</gui></title>
        <p>Wraps to <gui>Wrap Width</gui>, up to <gui>Max Lines</gui> lines,
        with <gui>Line Spacing</gui>, a <gui>Justification</gui> of
        <gui>Left</gui>, <gui>Centred</gui>, <gui>Right</gui> or
        <gui>Justified</gui>, and an <gui>Indent</gui> for every line after
        the first. The block is as tall as the lines the text needs, up to
        the maximum. A word too long for the width stays whole, on a line of
        its own.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Text block (^TB)</gui></title>
        <p>A box <gui>Wrap Width</gui> wide and <gui>Block Height</gui>
        tall. Its lines are spaced by the printer, about 1.28 times the font
        height apart, and text past the bottom is cut off. A word too long
        for the width is broken where it runs out of room. It breaks lines
        only at spaces, so a line break you type becomes a space. Its lines
        are left or right justified; a right justified block keeps its right
        edge still and grows to the left.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
    <p>Choose a field block for most wrapped text: it can centre or justify,
    and grows with the text. Choose a text block when the text must stay
    inside a fixed box whatever its length.</p>
    <note>
      <p>LinuxZPL leaves out lines past <gui>Max Lines</gui>. Zebra's manual
      says a printer prints the extra text over the last line instead, so
      set <gui>Max Lines</gui> high enough for all the text.</p>
    </note>
    <p>Dragging a block's handles makes its text bigger or smaller, as it
    does for a single line, and the block grows or shrinks with it, so the
    lines break in the same places and none of them is lost. The corner
    handles scale the whole block. The top and bottom handles make the
    letters taller or shorter. The side handles make the letters and the
    wrap width wider or narrower together. To change where the lines break,
    set <gui>Wrap Width</gui>, <gui>Max Lines</gui> or <gui>Block
    Height</gui> in the Properties panel.</p>
    <p>If you type more text than the block holds, the font gets smaller so
    that every line still fits. The block keeps its size, and a field block
    gets as many more lines as the smaller font makes room for. Deleting
    text does not make the font bigger again; set <gui>Font Height</gui> and
    <gui>Font Width</gui> for that. Clearing <gui>Wrap</gui> joins the lines
    back into one.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="time">
    <title>Dates and times from the printer's clock</title>
    <p><gui>+ Time</gui> adds text the printer fills in from its own clock as
    it prints (<code>^FC</code>). <gui>Format</gui> says what to print, using
    codes such as these:</p>
    <table frame="all" rules="rows">
      <thead>
        <tr><td><p>Code</p></td><td><p>Prints</p></td></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><p><code>%m/%d/%y</code></p></td><td><p>the date, as 11/05/26</p></td></tr>
        <tr><td><p><code>%H:%M:%S</code></p></td><td><p>the time, as 14:30:05</p></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The printer needs a real-time clock for this, which on some models is
    an option. Clearing <gui>Clock</gui> turns the field into ordinary
    text.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="serial">
    <title>Serial numbers</title>
    <p><gui>+ Serial</gui> adds a number the printer changes on every label
    it prints (<code>^SN</code>). <gui>Start Value</gui> is the first
    label's number, <gui>Increment</gui> what is added each time (a negative
    one counts down), and <gui>Add leading zeros</gui> keeps the number as
    many digits long as the start value. Clearing <gui>Serial</gui> turns
    the field into ordinary text.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="numbered">
    <title>Text filled in later</title>
    <p><gui>+ Numbered</gui> adds text whose words are given each time the
    label is printed (<code>^FN</code>), and the <gui>ID</gui> row names any
    text so a program can fill it in: see <link xref="templates"/>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="stand-ins">
    <title>What the canvas shows for fields the printer fills in</title>
    <p>The canvas can't know what the printer will put in these fields, so
    it shows a stand-in between « and »:</p>
    <list>
      <item><p>a date or time shows its format, such as
      <code>«%m/%d/%y»</code>;</p></item>
      <item><p>a serial number shows its start value and step, such as
      <code>0001«+1»</code>;</p></item>
      <item><p>a numbered field shows its field name, such as
      <code>«Ship to»</code>, or its number, such as
      <code>«FN4»</code>.</p></item>
    </list>
    <p>The box is the width of the stand-in, not of what will print, so leave
    room for the longest value the field will hold.</p>
  </section>
</page>
